  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="222e-931b-dca0-7a26" name="British Tribes (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #43 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). The Celtic communities in Britain had much in common with their continental brethren, but retained the use of chariots much later than elsewhere. They were able to give Julius Caesar a stiff fight but could not ultimately unite to repel the Romans once Claudius decided upon conquest. The failure of the Icenii revolt, and the subsequent Roman reprisals, marks the end of independent Celtic military power other than in Ireland and Northern Scotland. 

ARMY COMPOSITION:
[British Tribes]
  Cavalry and Chariots: up to 50%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 50%
  Skirmishers: up to 10%
  Characters: up to 25% Infantry: At least 50% Cavalry and Chariots: Up to 50% Skirmishers: Up to 10% Allies: None</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
